  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nDeep, occluding low pressure approaching Hudson Bay, with attendant \ncold frontal boundary draped SSW through Minnesota into Iowa. \nPinched pressure gradient between aforementioned surface low and \nstout area of surface high pressure over the eastern seaboard leaves \nus firmly within the return flow zone, leading to strong flow both \naloft and at the surface. Meager instability overhead, and \nsaturation continues to struggle per the 00z RAOB, showcasing \nstubborn low level dry air across the region. As such, current trend \nof meager convection, largely concentrated across the bottom couple \nrows of counties anticipated to continue into early tonight. \nReinforcements on the way, associated with a deeper convective \noutburst over Iowa, as that slug of moisture is forced into the \nregion as the cold front passes through this morning. Some areas may \nsee up to an inch of additional rainfall with this activity (and \nmodels seem to pinpoint the region between M-72 and M-68 for this, \nsubject to change), but overall, most spots likely see 0.10-0.25\", \nperhaps even less, especially if the dominant mode can remain \nconvective.\n\nThe uptick in convection / downpours should quickly depart as the \nfront clears the region. Drier air spills into the region through \nthe rest of the day, with winds turning more WSW to W in the wake of \nthe front. Continued pinched pressure gradient will lead to an \nuptick in winds, perhaps exceeding 35mph at times in eastern upper \nand along the lakeshores. Anticipating things to wind down a bit \ninto the overnight, though with a continued NW flow breeze. Cooler \nweather builds by Thursday as backside dry air firmly moves \noverhead. Highs likely struggle to break out of the mid to upper 60s \nfor most, with some 70s in the southern rows of counties. Ridging \nand surface high pressure builds overhead by Thursday afternoon \n(setting up for a chilly night Thursday night, potential for \ninterior lows in the upper 30s), amplified by another shortwave \ncrashing into the Pacific Northwest. This wave will have some pep in \nits step, racing into the region by Saturday, and drumming up return \nflow to nudge daytime highs back into the 70s and 80s Friday and \nSaturday. System passing through looks to be somewhat moisture \nstarved, but indications are there will be enough ingredients in \nplay to generate another chance of showers and thunder Saturday... \nthough coverage of this looks isolated to perhaps scattered at best. \nAnother area of high pressure builds in the wake of this system by \nSunday... accompanied by cooler and drier air before another system \npasses Monday (with more rain potential) and setting the stage for \nan autumnal stretch of weather into next week constituted by highs \nin the 60s to near 70 and lows in the 40s and 50s Tuesday and \nWednesday.",
